<section>
  <h2>Explanation</h2>
  <p>
    A particle moves on a line with a given velocity
    <i>v</i>(<i>t</i>) = <i>p</i> + <i>q t</i> + <i>r t</i>². Differentiate
    for acceleration and integrate for displacement from rest at the origin.
    <i>r</i> = 0 is constant acceleration; <i>r</i> = 0 and <i>q</i> = 0 is
    constant velocity.
  </p>
  <p>
    A quadratic velocity has a simpler derivative and a cubic integral.
    The constant of integration is fixed by <i>s</i> = 0 at <i>t</i> = 0.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><i>a</i>(<i>t</i>) = d<i>v</i>/d<i>t</i> = <i>q</i> + 2<i>r t</i></li>
    <li><i>s</i>(<i>t</i>) = ∫<i>v</i> d<i>t</i> = <i>p t</i> + ½<i>q t</i>² + ⅓<i>r t</i>³</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If <i>v</i> later becomes zero the particle is instantaneously at rest
    and then reverses, provided <i>a</i> is not also zero.
  </p>
</section>
